A previous attempt, pull request #364 on branch feature/a-failed-job-says-why, fixed two of the three defects well and replaced the third with a worse one. Read that diff before starting: take the parts named good, do not take the part named bad.

GOOD, and worth reusing. The vendor failure value is carried into a closed set instead of being discarded:

  case ManagementPlaneEnumerationResult.Failed failed -> EnumerationOutcome.failed(failureClass(failed.reason()));

  private static String failureClass(String reason) {
      return switch (reason) {
          case "unreachable" -> "UNREACHABLE";
          case "refused" -> "REFUSED";
          default -> "UNKNOWN_FAILURE";
      };
  }

and the reason now reaches the terminal write, and matched_expectation carries outcome.succeeded rather than null.

BAD, and the reason this movement exists:

  private static long measurementBytes(EnumerationOutcome outcome) {
      return outcome.toString().getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8).length;
  }
  private static long measurementLines(EnumerationOutcome outcome) {
      return outcome.toString().lines().count();
  }

output_bytes and output_lines are columns that say what the step SAW. These fill them with the length of a Java object's debug string. That is worse than the hardcoded zero it replaced: a zero is visibly wrong and invites the question, while a plausible number is read as a measurement and never questioned again.

The same hardcoded zeros exist at the other call site,
ui2/job-engine/src/main/java/.../executor/StepExecutor.java:263:

  attemptRepository.writeOutcome(attemptId, leaseEpoch, outcome, errorClass, 0L, 0L, ...)

The repository signature is
